---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Divider from '../components/Divider.astro';
import '../styles/landing.css';

const care = [
  {
    label: 'Care',
    badge: 'Managed — Monthly',
    price: '$250',
    tagline: 'Keep it running, updated, and secure.',
    features: [
      'Content updates & copy edits (text, images, small page changes)',
      'Uptime & security monitoring',
      'Software/dependency updates',
      "Monthly check-in email confirming everything's healthy",
      'Includes 2 hrs/month; overage billed at $125/hr',
    ],
    cta: 'Start a Conversation',
  },
  {
    label: 'Priority Care',
    badge: 'Managed — Monthly',
    price: '$750',
    tagline: 'Faster response, more room to build.',
    features: [
      'Everything in Care, plus:',
      'Small feature additions & integration tweaks',
      'Priority response (next business day)',
      'Quarterly performance/strategy check-in call',
      'Includes 6 hrs/month; overage billed at $125/hr',
    ],
    cta: 'Book a Discovery Call',
  },
];
---

<Layout title="Small Business Website Design" description="Small business website design and development in Wichita, KS. Custom or Webflow sites you can edit yourself, from one local developer. Free intake call.">

<!-- ── PAGE HERO ── -->
<section class="page-hero">
  <div class="page-hero-grid"></div>
  <div class="page-hero-glow"></div>
  <div class="page-hero-inner">
    <div class="page-eyebrow fade-up d1">
      <span class="eyebrow-num">// Web &amp; CMS</span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="page-headline fade-up d2">
      Small Business<br>Website Design<br><em>in Wichita</em>
    </h1>
    <p class="page-sub fade-up d3">
      A website built to be easy for you to update, by one person you can reach directly.
      Custom-coded or on Webflow, for small businesses in Wichita and the towns around it.
    </p>
    <div class="page-hero-meta fade-up d4">
      <div class="meta-item"><span class="meta-dot"></span>Wichita, KS</div>
      <div class="meta-divider"></div>
      <div class="meta-item"><span class="meta-dot"></span>Webflow or Custom Code</div>
      <div class="meta-divider"></div>
      <div class="meta-item"><span class="meta-dot"></span>Free Intake Call</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── SUMMARY + CONTENTS ── -->
<div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
  <div class="section">
    <div class="lp-intro">
      <div class="lp-card reveal">
        <div class="lp-card-label">// The short version</div>
        <ul class="lp-summary-list">
          <li><strong>Who it's for</strong>Small businesses and professional-service firms in Wichita, KS and the towns around it.</li>
          <li><strong>What you get</strong>A custom-coded or Webflow website that works well on phones and is built to be easy for you to update.</li>
          <li><strong>How it works</strong>A free 30-minute intake call, a written scope-of-work plan, a working preview you review, then launch.</li>
          <li><strong>What it costs</strong>Smaller single-scope projects run $500–$1,500. Larger builds are quoted after the intake call. Optional care plans are $250 or $750 a month.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="reveal">
        <div class="lp-card-label">// In this page</div>
        <ol class="lp-toc">
          <li><a href="#needs">What a small business website needs</a></li>
          <li><a href="#tools">Webflow, custom code, and who owns what</a></li>
          <li><a href="#process">How a project works</a></li>
          <li><a href="#example">A recent example</a></li>
          <li><a href="#cost">Cost, and what happens after launch</a></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── 01 WHAT A SITE NEEDS ── -->
<div id="needs" class="lp-anchor" style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header reveal">
      <span class="section-num">01</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">What a Small Business<br><em>Website Needs</em></h2>
    </div>
    <Divider variant="angular" />

    <div class="lp-prose reveal">
      <p>
        A small business website has a few jobs. It has to look credible to someone who has never
        heard of you, load quickly on a phone, and make it obvious how to get in touch. Everything
        else is secondary. When I do small business website design in Wichita, I start from those
        jobs and work outward, rather than starting from a template and hoping it fits.
      </p>
      <p>
        The most common mistake I see is an idea for the site that is too grand. If you're a small
        business without a website, the main goal is to get one live and let it mature, so it
        becomes more searchable over time. Build what you need for where you are now. A site can
        always be scaled up later, but an overly large site that is hard to manage becomes a
        headache and often gets abandoned.
      </p>
      <p>
        In practice that means a mobile-first layout, which is a design that starts with the phone
        screen and then scales up to desktop. It means search basics done properly: clear page
        titles and descriptions, which are the text search engines show for your pages. And it
        means attention to performance, including Core Web Vitals, which are Google's measures of
        how fast and stable a page feels while it loads.
      </p>
      <p>
        I'm a small business web developer in Wichita, KS, and you work directly with me, the
        person doing the building. I serve Wichita, El Dorado, Augusta, Hutchinson, Mount Hope,
        Cheney, Maize, Bel Aire, Andover, Goddard, Park City, and Derby.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── 02 TOOLS AND OWNERSHIP ── -->
<div id="tools" class="lp-anchor" style="background: var(--bg2); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header reveal">
      <span class="section-num">02</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">Webflow, Custom Code,<br><em>and Who Owns What</em></h2>
    </div>
    <Divider variant="angular" />

    <div class="lp-prose reveal">
      <p>
        Which tool is right depends on the use case. Template-based builders are quick to start
        with, but they can limit how much you can customize as your needs grow. Custom code allows
        anything, because the site is written from scratch. Webflow is a middle ground: a visual
        website builder that gives you a shell to work in without restricting creativity. A Webflow
        site can include a CMS, short for content management system, which is the editing area
        behind the site where you change text, images, and pages without touching code.
      </p>
      <p>
        For my own projects I prefer code, for the freedom. But a case can be made for any of these
        tools depending on the situation, so I keep all of them in my toolbox and adapt to each
        client instead of pushing one.
      </p>
      <p>
        I usually build a working preview site first, hosted locally or on a service like Netlify,
        so you and I can iterate together. You approve the copy and see how the pages flow before
        anything goes live. At handoff you receive the design files, the code, and the hosting
        setup. I usually ask you to buy and set up your own domain, which is your web address, so
        it is yours from the start.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── 03 HOW A PROJECT WORKS ── -->
<div id="process" class="lp-anchor" style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header reveal">
      <span class="section-num">03</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">How a Project<br><em>Works</em></h2>
    </div>
    <Divider variant="angular" />

    <div class="lp-prose reveal">
      <p>
        It starts with a message, through the contact form or by email. Email is the best way to
        reach me, and I reply within 24 hours.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then we have an informal intake call. It is free and takes about 30 minutes. We talk through
        your vision and where you are right now, including whether you've already bought a domain or
        hosting. We cover any style or color palette you like, whether you want basic SEO, and
        whether this is a one-time build or you'll want managed services afterward.
      </p>
      <p>
        After the call I turn what we discussed into a written scope-of-work plan for you to review.
        If the scope changes later, we agree on the change in writing before any work begins, so
        there are no surprise charges. Revisions within the agreed scope are included.
      </p>
      <p>
        A full website build generally starts at about 6 weeks, depending on scope. That doesn't
        include your review time. I ask that review feedback come back within about 15 days,
        though I'm flexible. A single scoped deliverable, such as a landing page, has a 1–2 week
        delivery window. At the end you get documentation and training, so you know how to update
        the site yourself.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── 04 A RECENT EXAMPLE ── -->
<div id="example" class="lp-anchor" style="background: var(--bg2); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header reveal">
      <span class="section-num">04</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">A Recent<br><em>Example</em></h2>
    </div>
    <Divider variant="angular" />

    <div class="lp-prose reveal">
      <p>
        I rebuilt the website for a Wichita law firm on Webflow. The work covered a new site
        structure, a mobile-first layout across screen sizes, a CMS the firm's own team can update,
        attorney profiles, practice-area pages, a blog, and training with written handoff
        documentation.
      </p>
      <p>
        I prioritized the organization of the site's assets and CMS entries, because the previous
        site's assets and content entries were disorganized. I created new pages from scratch, and
        I added the ability to post jobs and receive resumes directly through the site. You can see
        more on the <a href="/portfolio/">portfolio page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="lp-note reveal">
      <span class="lp-note-label">// Independence</span>
      <p>
        DataForge is a separate, independent company, and nothing on this page speaks for any
        law firm.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── 05 COST AND AFTER LAUNCH ── -->
<div id="cost" class="lp-anchor" style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header reveal">
      <span class="section-num">05</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">Cost, and What Happens<br><em>After Launch</em></h2>
    </div>
    <Divider variant="angular" />

    <div class="lp-prose reveal" style="margin-bottom:48px;">
      <p>
        Smaller, single-scope projects fall in the Starter range: <strong>$500–$1,500 per
        project</strong>, with a 1–2 week delivery window, revisions within the agreed scope, email
        support, and handoff documentation. Larger site builds are quoted after the intake call.
        While a larger build is actively underway, a monthly arrangement is available at
        $1,500–$3,000 a month. Full details are on the <a href="/services/#pricing">Services page</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        After launch, managed services are available through two Care plans, one with a priority
        response time and one without. They are for sites I've built, or others after a quick
        review. The first is $250 a month. The second is $750 a month and adds a faster response.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="pricing-grid pricing-grid--retainer">
      {care.map((r) => (
        <div class="pricing-card reveal">
          <div class="pricing-featured-bar pricing-featured-bar--retainer"></div>
          <div class="pricing-badge">{r.badge}</div>
          <div class="pricing-label">{r.label}</div>
          <div class="pricing-price">
            {r.price}
            <span class="pricing-cadence">/month</span>
          </div>
          <div class="pricing-tagline">{r.tagline}</div>
          <div class="pricing-divider"></div>
          <ul class="pricing-features">
            {r.features.map((f) => (
              <li>
                <svg width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 12 12" fill="none">
                  <path d="M2 6l3 3 5-5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                </svg>
                {f}
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
          <a href="/contact/" class="btn-ghost pricing-cta">{r.cta}</a>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="lp-prose reveal">
      <p>
        Unused hours don't roll over, and I always confirm extra time before any extra work starts.
        Email is the best way to reach me, and I reply within 24 hours.
      </p>
      <p>
        Many small businesses find that their next problem sits behind the website: manual
        processes, scattered spreadsheets, and reports someone builds by hand each month. As a
        business systems consultant in Wichita, I also build automation, dashboards, and process
        cleanups; see <a href="/services/">all services</a>. If you run a law firm, see
        <a href="/law-firm-technology-consulting-wichita/">law firm technology consulting in Wichita</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── CTA STRIP ── -->
<div class="cta-strip">
  <div class="cta-glow"></div>
  <div class="cta-eyebrow">// Start Here</div>
  <h2 class="cta-headline">Let's Talk<br><em>About Your Site.</em></h2>
  <p class="cta-sub">
    Tell me about your business and what you want the site to do. Every project starts with a
    free 30-minute intake call.
  </p>
  <div class="cta-actions">
    <a href="/contact/" class="btn-primary">Book a Free Intake Call</a>
    <a href="/services/" class="btn-ghost">See Services &amp; Pricing</a>
  </div>
</div>

</Layout>
